<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banavasi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Banavasi is an ancient temple town in Uttara <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the Varada river. Mentioned in early Buddhist sources, it was a centre of the Chutu rulers and then, from the mid-4th century, the capital of the Kadamba dynasty, the first native dynasty to rule Karnataka. Its Madhukeshwara temple and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> inscriptions recall its long history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banavasi.jpg" alt="Banavasi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shashidhara halady · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banavasi_Madhukeshwara_temple.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chalukyas of Badami rose in the mid-sixth century in the valley of the Malaprabha River in northern Karnataka, a region previously dominated by the Kadamba dynasty of <b>Banavasi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Krishna valley the Ikshvaku dynasty ruled from Vijayapuri, at Nagarjunakonda, and continued the lavish patronage of Buddhist monasteries; the Chutu dynasty held the south-west around <b>Banavasi</b>; and later the Pallava dynasty in the south-east and the Vakataka dynasty in the north inherited much of the former Satavahana sphere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The king ruled as maharajadhiraja from a court that combined Sanskrit learning with Kannada administration, and princes were often sent as viceroys to frontier regions; Vikramaditya VI himself governed <b>Banavasi</b> before taking the throne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Satavahana dynasty declined in the 2nd and 3rd centuries, local rulers called Chutus took over the region around <b>Banavasi</b> in northern Karnataka and parts of the coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/chutu-dynasty">Chutu dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>He took up arms, gathered forest chiefs and forced the Pallava dynasty to recognise him as ruler of the region around <b>Banavasi</b> in the mid-4th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kadamba-dynasty">Kadamba dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The Goa Kadambas claimed descent from the earlier Kadamba dynasty of <b>Banavasi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kadambas-of-goa">Kadambas of Goa</a></li></ul></section>
